    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d884">

                <pb facs="0810" n="798" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg798"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.Consultation (2)/334</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Guatemala</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Cabot</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1356</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-07-11">July 11,
                        1940</date>.<lb/>[Received July 16.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my telegram No. 67 of
                    July 11, 5 [<hi rendition="#italic">3</hi>] P.M., 1940, I have the honor to
                    enclose herewith a copy and translation of the Note which the German Minister
                    forwarded to the Guatemalan Foreign Office under date of July 1st with regard to
                    the forthcoming Consultative Meeting at Habana.</p>
                <p>As reported in the above-mentioned telegram, I discovered when checking up the
                    newspaper story mentioned by the Department, that a copy of this Note had
                    already been forwarded to me by the Foreign Office that morning and, in fact, it
                    reached me shortly after midday. I do not know why the Foreign Office should
                    have sent a copy of this Note to this Legation ten days after it was written,
                    but presume that it must have been done on the basis of the newspaper story
                    similar to that mentioned in the Department’s telegram which was published in
                    the local newspapers on the evening of July 10th, under date line of San José,
                    Costa Rica.</p>
                <p>The German Minister’s Note does not appear to call for any particular comment. It
                    is couched in the same arrogant terms as other <pb facs="0811" n="799"
                        xml:id="pg799"/> recent communications to the Guatemalan Government in
                    connection with acts of the latter which have been distasteful to the German
                    Government (see particularly this Legation’s despatch No. 1268 of May 22, 1940,
                    file No. 711.1/820.02<anchor n="52a" xml:id="d884-f1"/>).</p>
                <p>As mentioned in my telegram under reference, I hope to be given a copy of the
                    German protest to the Foreign Office in regard to Alfredo Schlesinger’s recently
                    published pamphlet, <hi rendition="#italic">El Arma Secreta: La Quinta
                        Columna</hi>, (see despatch No. 1348 of July 6, 1940, file No. 820.02<anchor
                        n="53" xml:id="d884-f2"/>), and if it is given to me I shall forward it in a
                    separate despatch.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">John</hi> M. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Cabot</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d884-a1.xml" xpointer="d884-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
